
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Noting a consequence: dropping comment events removes the comments subsystem from scope entirely, and it simplifies
  implicit subscription to [1mcreator + assignee[0m — there's no "commenter" relationship left to track. That's a real scope
  win.

  Last big fork: [1mdelivery.[0m In-app is obviously slice one. The question is whether the design treats delivery as a
  pluggable channel from the start or hardcodes it.

  - [1mIn-app only, hardcoded[0m — a bell icon, an unread list, done. Buys the smallest possible thing that works. Costs:
    adding email later touches the notification-creation path, because "create a notification" and "show it in the UI"
    end up as one step.
  - [1mChannel abstraction, one channel implemented[0m — creating a notification is separate from delivering it; in-app is a
    channel that happens to be the only registered one. Buys a clean seam where email/push slot in without touching
    event handling. Costs: one extra indirection you can't justify from slice one alone.
  - [1mIn-app + email now[0m — real cross-channel from the start. Costs: email needs a server, a sending provider,
    deliverability handling, digest batching (nobody wants one email per status change), and unsubscribe links. That's a
    project of its own, and it needs the backend that doesn't exist yet.

  I recommend the [1mchannel abstraction with only in-app implemented[0m. It's the cheap version of the expensive decision:
  the indirection is maybe twenty lines now, whereas retrofitting it means reopening every call site that creates a
  notification. Email stays explicitly out of scope until there's a server to send from.

